--TEST--
all four of `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R11's grammars refuse a tainted argument, and they agree across four classes rather than each answering for itself — `rule:security/sink-predicate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/sink-predicate` classifies a parameter whose content becomes an instruction as
// a sink, and `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R11 names the four grammars that are one: a regex
// pattern, a `printf` template, a CLDR date pattern and a `Core\Bytes::pack`
// format. Each already has cases of its own; what this file asks is that they
// **agree** -- a member that grew its own answer here still reads right on its
// own line and fails on this one.
tainted string $t = "a";

// The pattern grammar, at the member that compiles one.
Core\Regex\Pattern $p = Core\Regex::compile($t);

// And at the `Pattern|string` position every other `Core\Regex` member takes,
// which is the same sink one union deep.
array<string> $parts = Core\Regex::split("a-b", $t);

// The `printf` template. Its variadic arguments are `mixed` and are not this
// row's business; the template itself is.
string $formatted = Core\Str::format($t, 1);

// The pack format, from a different class entirely.
bytes $packed = Core\Bytes::pack($t, 1);

// The CLDR date pattern, on an instance member.
string $rendered = Core\Time\Date::at(2024, 1, 1)->format($t);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

